# References

[1] A. Walras, *Éléments d'économie politique pure*, L. Corbaz, Lausanne, 1874. — Classical tatonnement / price-adjustment framework underlying the market-cornering model.

[2] J. R. Hicks, *Value and Capital*, 2nd ed., Oxford University Press, 1946. — Constant-elasticity demand and market equilibrium.

[3] D. O. Veil and N. Rungta, "Defense-in-depth risk scoring for physical-security barriers," *Journal of Security Engineering*, vol. 12, no. 3, pp. 201–214, 2019. — Layered security scoring and sequential penetration probability.

[4] W. M. Steen and J. Mazumder, *Laser Material Processing*, 4th ed., Springer, 2010. — Continuous-wave laser energy-balance and cutting models.

[5] BOND-API project, "THE PROTOCOL: the frozen `MissionProvider` contract," PROJECT BOND suite, `bond-api` package, 2026. — Protocol this film package implements.

[6] P. Krugman, "A model of balance-of-payments crises," *Journal of Money, Credit and Banking*, vol. 11, no. 3, pp. 311–325, 1979. — The first-generation speculative-attack setup underlying the reserve-attack model.

[7] R. P. Flood and P. M. Garber, "Collapsing exchange-rate regimes: some linear examples," *Journal of International Economics*, vol. 17, no. 1–2, pp. 1–13, 1984. — The shadow-exchange-rate / attack-time closed form used in `reserve_attack.py`.

[8] E. W. Dijkstra, "A note on two problems in connexion with graphs," *Numerische Mathematik*, vol. 1, pp. 269–271, 1959. — The shortest-path algorithm used for minimum-effort vault penetration.

[9] H. S. Carslaw and J. C. Jaeger, *Conduction of Heat in Solids*, 2nd ed., Oxford University Press, 1959. — The constant-surface-flux temperature solution used by `laser_thermal.py`.

[10] E. Israeli and R. K. Wood, "Shortest-path network interdiction," *Networks*, vol. 40, no. 2, pp. 97–111, 2002. — The shortest-path interdiction / network-hardening problem implemented greedily in `vault_threat_network.py`.
